# Backend Implementation

## Overview

The backend will replace the existing PHP backend and provide the services required by the React frontend.

The new backend will use:

- **Node.js** — Backend runtime
- **TypeScript** — Primary development language
- **MySQL** — Relational database
- **Prisma ORM** — Database access, schema management, and migrations
- **REST API** — Communication between the React frontend and backend

The goal is to create a secure, maintainable, and scalable backend while preserving the functionality required by the existing application.

---

## Architecture

```text
React + TypeScript + Ant Design
              |
              v
          REST API
              |
              v
       Node.js + TypeScript
              |
              v
           Prisma
              |
              v
            MySQL
```

The React frontend will **not** connect directly to the MySQL database. All database operations will go through the Node.js API.

This allows the backend to centrally handle:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Role-based permissions
- Business rules
- Data validation
- Database operations
- Error handling
- Security

---

## Role-Based Access Control

The backend will use a **role-based access control (RBAC)** system.

Permissions will be enforced on the backend, not only hidden in the frontend. This prevents users from bypassing frontend restrictions by manually calling API endpoints.

### Roles

| Role | Permissions |
|---|---|
| **Super Admin** | Full system access. Can create, read, update, delete, and manage users and other administrative resources. |
| **Admin** | Can manage users and tours according to the permissions assigned to the Admin role. |
| **Tour Guide** | Can manage tours assigned to them. Cannot manage tours belonging to other tour guides and does not have full administrative access. |

### Tour Guide Ownership

Tour Guides will have ownership restrictions.

For example:

```text
Tour Guide A
    └── Tour A

Tour Guide B
    └── Tour B
```

Tour Guide A can manage **Tour A**, but cannot modify or delete **Tour B**.

These restrictions will be enforced by the backend.

